---
title: "The competition"
description: "The scoring model as a participant experiences it: four tiers, a weekly target, per-member guild ranking, and why nothing is ever totalled."
---

Before any code, the product. Every design decision in the repository is
downstream of this model, so a change that looks harmless in code is often a
change to the competition's rules.

## What a person does

Once a day, at an hour they chose, the bot sends one message: **"Moved today?"**
Under it are four buttons.

| Button label | Tier | Minutes credited |
|---|---|---|
| 15 to 30 min | `short` | 22 |
| 30 to 60 min | `medium` | 45 |
| 60+ min | `long` | 75 |
| Not today | `rest` | 0 |

The button says a **range** and the system credits a **single number** inside it.
That gap is the whole point: the user is never asked to be precise, and the
credited value is the same for everyone who taps the same button, so no amount of
careful self-reporting can earn an advantage.

The tap is the commit. There is no confirmation step, no duration field, no sport
picker, and no tags. `SPEC.md` §8 records the sport taxonomy and MET-based
scoring as explicitly rejected: they produce arguments about whether bouldering
beats swimming, and the competition needs participation rather than precision.

Three properties of this list are deliberate and easy to erode:

- **Four options, one tap.** The whole product hinges on the daily cost being one
  tap. Any fifth thing to decide is a change to that.
- **Rest is a real record, not an absence.** FR-8. It suppresses the next day's
  reminder and counts toward the guild's participation percentage, so an honest
  rest day is rewarded rather than punished. A user who logs nothing at all is a
  different state from a user who logged rest.
- **The top tier is capped at 75 minutes.** Not because nobody trains longer, but
  because the health benefit plateaus around 300 minutes a week and the cap bounds
  dishonesty to roughly three times an honest report. See `src/config.ts:39-43`.

## The personal target

150 minutes a week, the WHO guideline for adults aged 18 to 64. Every
confirmation message shows progress against it as a ten-slot bar.

The target exists so there is a win available to everyone that does not require
beating anybody. A person in the ninth-placed guild can still hit their own
target every week, and the streak counter in `/me` counts exactly that.

**The streak is counted in weeks, never days.** A daily streak would teach that a
rest day is a failure, which contradicts the rest button existing at all. The
current week counts toward the streak only once its target is already met, so a
week still in progress never breaks a streak the user has not had time to extend
yet (`src/domain/scoring.ts:75-97`).

## How guilds are ranked

**Minutes per member, across the guild's entire roster**, including every member
who has never opened the bot.

$$
\text{score}_{\text{guild}} = \frac{\sum \text{minutes logged by registered members}}{\text{configured member count}}
$$

The denominator is the guild's real size from `src/config.ts`, not its number of
registered users. This is the single most consequential choice in the model, and
it has two consequences worth internalising before you change anything near it:

1. **Activating a quiet member is worth more than pushing an active one harder.**
   A guild cannot win by having ten very committed people. The strategy the
   scoring rewards is recruitment inside the guild.
2. **A wrong member count silently distorts everything.** It is a denominator, so
   there is no error, no warning, and no visible symptom: just a guild that is
   quietly ranked wrong all season. The counts in `src/config.ts` are carried over
   from `SPEC.md` §1 and are **unverified**. See [Next season](../ops/next-season.tmd).

Two tables are published, always in this order: **this week first, then the
season**. That order is deliberate (FR-16). A guild sitting ninth for the season
can still be winning the current week, and leading with the cumulative table
would give the bottom guilds nothing but a repeated failure signal. `SPEC.md` §11
rates a guild disengaging from a hopeless position as the competition's top risk.

Ties share the best rank, so the table reads 1, 1, 3 rather than 1, 2, 3
(`competitionRanks` in `src/domain/scoring.ts:33-49`). Before anyone has logged
anything, every guild is jointly first rather than arbitrarily ordered.

## What each surface shows

| Surface | Who sees it | What it contains |
|---|---|---|
| Check-in message | one person, privately | the four tier buttons, plus "Log yesterday instead" |
| Confirmation | one person, privately | minutes credited, weekly progress bar, Undo |
| `/me` | one person, privately | weekly progress, streak, their guild's rank, and up to three "Around you" rows |
| `/standings` | anyone, anywhere | the two guild tables. No personal data at all |
| Pinned message | a guild chat | the same two tables, edited in place every 15 minutes, silently |
| Monday post | a guild chat | last week's winner, the reader's guild's placing and participation share. The one interruption per week |

**There is no individual leaderboard anywhere, and adding one is a defect** (FR-15,
`SPEC.md` §8). The "Around you" block in `/me` is the permitted half of that
requirement: the user plus at most one person either side of them, inside their
own guild only, capped at three rows. No query in the codebase returns a top-N
list of individuals, and the cap is what keeps it that way.

This is also why `/me` refuses to answer in a group chat: the "Around you" block
names other people. `/standings` is deliberately unguarded, because it carries
only guild totals.

## The competition window

The competition runs between two dates in `src/config.ts`, inclusive, in
`Europe/Helsinki`. Outside it, nothing is written at all (FR-26): a tap on a
stale button lands on a refusal rather than on a row nobody will ever count.

The window straddles the October clock change, which is why every date bucket in
the system is computed by Postgres rather than by JavaScript date arithmetic. See
[The database](../code/database.tmd).

## Reminders, in one paragraph

At registration the user is asked, once, whether they want a daily nudge and at
what hour (17, 18, 20 or 21). If they decline, they are never asked again. If
they accept, the bot sends the check-in message at that hour, and only if they
have not already recorded that day. After **five consecutive ignored reminders**
the daily send stops and the bot spends exactly one message asking whether to
continue. If that goes unanswered too, the user is paused indefinitely.

Crucially, **logging does not un-pause them**. The bot asked for consent and got
none, and logging is engagement with the competition rather than consent to be
messaged. Only tapping "Keep them" or running `/remind` resumes. This asymmetry is
FR-22 read strictly, and it is easy to "fix" by accident. See
[Invariants](../invariants.tmd).
